   148	\subsection{Background}
   149	
   150	Write $L_N[\alpha,c]=\exp\bigl(c(\log N)^\alpha(\log\log N)^{1-\alpha}\bigr)$ and $L=L_N[1/2,1]$. The
   151	fastest factoring algorithms in practice, the quadratic and number field sieves, run in heuristic time
   152	$L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]$ and $L_N[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}+o(1)]$
   153	\cite{pomerance1982,lllm1990,blp1993}. Their running times are not proved: they rest on assumptions that
   154	certain polynomial values behave like random integers with respect to smoothness, and that the
   155	resulting congruences of squares are not all trivial.
   156	
   157	Rigorous analysis has a separate history.
   158	\begin{itemize}
   159	\item Dixon's random-squares method \cite{dixon1981} was the first factoring algorithm with a proved
   160	  subexponential running time, $L_N[1/2,O(1)]$. Its correctness argument is simple and is the starting point of
   161	  this paper. Draw $r$ uniformly, look for relations $r^2\equiv\prod p^{e_p}\pmod N$ over a factor base,
   162	  and combine them into $X^2\equiv Y^2$. Since $Y$ is computed without looking at the roots $r$, the
   163	  class of $X/Y$ in the group of square roots of unity is uniformly distributed, and $\gcd(X-Y,N)$ is
   164	  a proper factor with probability at least $1/2$. This is the same fibre symmetry that underlies
   165	  Rabin's reduction from computing square roots to factoring \cite{rabin1979}.
   166	\item Pomerance analysed and refined these methods \cite{pomerance1982}, and proved $L_N[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]$ for
   167	  random squares with a rigorous elliptic curve smoothness test \cite{pomerance1987}. Vallée's
   168	  sampler of small squares gave $L_N[1/2,\sqrt{4/3}+o(1)]$ \cite{vallee1991}.
   169	\item Class-group methods reached exponent $1$. Schnorr and Lenstra \cite{schnorrlenstra1984} did so under
   170	  heuristic hypotheses. Seysen \cite{seysen1987} gave a class-group algorithm rigorous under the generalised Riemann hypothesis, and A.~K.~Lenstra
   171	  \cite{aklenstra1988} brought the GRH-conditional constant to $1$. Lenstra and Pomerance
   172	  \cite{lp1992} removed every hypothesis. They proved that the complete prime factorisation of every
   173	  $N$ can be found in expected time $L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]$. That bound has stood since 1992. It is still
   174	  described as the best rigorous probabilistic bound, for instance by Pomerance \cite{pomerance2008}
   175	  and Harvey \cite{harvey2021}.
   176	\item Lee and Venkatesan \cite{lv2018} analysed rigorously an explicitly randomised number field sieve. They proved that it finds
   177	  congruences of squares in expected time $L_N[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}+o(1)]$. Whether these congruences yield
   178	  a factor is left to a conjecture (their Conjecture~7.1), and their factoring conclusion is
   179	  stated for $N=pq$ with $p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod 4$. Their techniques for the first moment of smooth
   180	  values (a randomisation that makes algebraic values nearly uniform on long progressions, and a restricted
   181	  Bombieri--Vinogradov estimate for smooth moduli built on Harper's work \cite{harper2012}) and for the post-processing
   182	  (random characters, Stark--Landau) are the models for ours. We use their randomisation idea and the Stark and Landau
   183	  statements as quoted there, but none of their lemmas on smooth numbers.
   184	\end{itemize}
   185	There is also a folklore heuristic that $L[1/2,1]$ is a natural barrier for methods that combine random-like
   186	auxiliary integers of size at least $N^{1/2}$ into a square. Croot, Granville, Pemantle and Tetali
   187	\cite{cgpt2012} and Balister, Bollob\'as and Morris \cite{bbm2018} made it sharp for random integers. We found no
   188	theorem showing that $c=1$ is a barrier for rigorous factoring itself.
   189	
   190	\subsection{The result}
   191	
   192	All our parameters are fixed: an odd degree $d\ge3$, an integer $k>(d+1)/4$, a rational base exponent
   193	$\mu$ and a rational slack $\eta_0>0$. Put $\nu=1/(4k)$ and
   194	\begin{equation}\label{eq:theta}
   195	h_*(\mu)=\max\bigl(2\nu,\ \mu-(d-2)\nu,\ 1-d\mu+\nu\bigr),\qquad
   196	\theta=\theta(d,k,\mu,\eta_0)=2\mu+h_*(\mu)+\eta_0+(d-1)\nu .
   197	\end{equation}
   198	The parameters are \emph{admissible} if $\nu+\eta_0\le\mu<1$ and $\theta<1$.
   199	
   200	\begin{theorem}[main theorem, fixed parameters]\label{thm:main}
   201	For every admissible $(d,k,\mu,\eta_0)$ there is a Las Vegas algorithm which, given any integer $N\ge2$,
   202	outputs the complete prime factorisation of $N$. Its expected running time is at most
   203	\[
   204	L_N\bigl[1/2,\ (1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)\bigr]\qquad(N\to\infty),
   205	\]
   206	where the $o(1)$ depends only on $(d,k,\mu,\eta_0)$.
   207	\end{theorem}
   208	
   209	Minimising $\theta$ over $\mu$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:optmu}) gives, at $\eta_0\downarrow0$,
   210	\[
   211	\theta_{\min}(d,k)=\begin{cases}
   212	\dfrac2d+\dfrac{d^2+d-2}{d}\,\nu,& k<(d^2+1)/4,\\[2mm]
   213	\dfrac3{d+1}+\dfrac{4d-2}{d+1}\,\nu,& k\ge(d^2+1)/4,
   214	\end{cases}
   215	\qquad
   216	\inf_{k,\eta_0}\frac{1+\theta}{\sqrt2}=\frac{1+3/(d+1)}{\sqrt2}.
   217	\]
   218	Letting $d$ grow gives the uniform statement.
   219	
   220	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:main}
   221	For every $c>1/\sqrt2$ there is a Las Vegas algorithm which, given any integer $N\ge2$, outputs its
   222	complete prime factorisation in expected time $O(L_N[1/2,c])$.
   223	\end{corollary}
   224	
   225	\begin{proof}
   226	Choose $d$ with $(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2<c$, then $k$ large and $\eta_0$ small with $(1+\theta)/\sqrt2<c$, and apply
   227	Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. Finitely many $N$ cost $O(1)$.
   228	\end{proof}
   229	
   230	Table~\ref{tab:constants} lists exact values. The constant is below $1$ if and only if $\theta<\sqrt2-1=0.41421\ldots$.
