  2023	\section{Uniformity, small factors and prime powers; proof of the main theorem}\label{sec:uniform}
  2024	%======================================================================
  2025	
  2026	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
  2027	\emph{Correctness.} Every prime in the output is certified by AKS. Every proper factor returned by SPLIT, or by a factor exit,
  2028	divides the current integer. FACTOR recurses on both cofactors and stops at certified primes. So the output is the complete
  2029	factorisation, and the algorithm is Las Vegas: it never errs, only its running time is random.
  2030	
  2031	\emph{Preprocessing.} Step~1 of FACTOR (primality, perfect powers) costs $(\log N)^{O(1)}$. Trial division to $B(N)$ costs $L^{a+o(1)}$. The cofactor
  2032	$N'$ handed to SPLIT is odd, has no prime factor $\le B(N)$, and is neither a prime nor a perfect power, so $\omega(N')\ge2$. Since
  2033	$B(N')\le B(N)$, $N'$ has no prime factor $\le B(N')$, and all statements of Sections~\ref{sec:split}--\ref{sec:cost} apply to $N'$ with its own
  2034	parameters. A non-squarefree $N'$ (for example $p^2q$) needs no special treatment: Theorem~\ref{thm:DD} uses $|\bmu|=2^{\omega}$, and the other
  2035	sections use only $\gcd(N',t)=1$ and $N'\mid F_A(g_0,g_1)$.
  2036	
  2037	\emph{Small $N'$.} Step~3 of FACTOR runs SPLIT and trial division in parallel, so its cost is at most twice the smaller of their costs. For
  2038	$N'<N_0$, trial division bounds it by $O(\sqrt{N_0})=O(1)$. For $N'\ge N_0$, Proposition~\ref{prop:cost} bounds it by
  2039	$L_{N'}^{(1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)}\le L_N^{(1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)}$. Here $N_0=N_0(d,k,\mu,\eta_0)$ is effective provided the constants $\eta_D,c_D,\delta_D$ of Fact~\ref{fact:D} are (Remark~\ref{rem:noN0});
  2040	it involves the effective constants of
  2041	Facts~\ref{fact:Stark}--\ref{fact:LO} and of the smooth-number facts, but it need not be known to the algorithm.
  2042	
  2043	\emph{Recursion.} Every call of SPLIT produces a proper factor, and the factorisation tree has fewer than $\log_2N$ internal nodes. So
  2044	there are at most $\log_2N$ calls of SPLIT and of trial division, each on an integer $\le N$, plus polynomial bookkeeping. The bound of
  2045	Proposition~\ref{prop:cost} is uniform in the input: write its bound as $L_n^{c+\epsilon(n)}$ with $c=(1+\theta)/\sqrt2$ and $\epsilon(n)\to0$. For
  2046	fixed $\epsilon>0$ there is $n_\epsilon$ with $\epsilon(n)\le\epsilon$ for $n\ge n_\epsilon$. So every call on an $N'\le N$ costs at most
  2047	$C_\epsilon+L_N^{c+\epsilon}$, since $L_{N'}\le L_N$. Summing over at most $\log_2N$ calls, the expected total time is at most
  2048	$\log_2N\,(C_\epsilon+L_N^{c+\epsilon})$ for every $\epsilon>0$, that is, $L_N^{c+o(1)}$.
  2049	
  2050	\emph{Uniformity.} All $L^{o(1)}$ factors are uniform in $N$: $(\log N)^{O(k)}$, $\binom{2k}k$, $2^{O(\omega(N))}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:omega}),
  2051	$\gamma^{-O(k)}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:Dk}), and the Wiedemann and Hensel polylogarithms. No exceptional set of $N$ arises. The analytic inputs need
  2052	only $\gcd(N,t)=1$, the absence of prime factors $\le B$, and the coprimality statements of Lemma~\ref{lem:coprime}. They need no squarefreeness
  2053	and no condition on the residue classes of the prime factors of $N$.
  2054	\end{proof}
  2055	
  2056	\begin{remark}[expected time versus probability]
  2057	Every loop of the algorithm is Las Vegas with verified outputs: primes are certified, kernel vectors checked, $\eta^2=\Gamma$ tested, and gcds
  2058	checked. So only expected costs have to be bounded. The Lenstra--Pomerance algorithm is used only through its expected-time bound, so
  2059	no cap on its running time is needed.
  2060	\end{remark}
