   602	larger of the first two. The third meets the first at $\mu=(1-\nu)/d$ and the second at
   603	$\mu=(1+(d-1)\nu)/(d+1)$. Comparing the first two there, the first is the larger iff $\mu\le d\nu$, which
   604	at $\mu=(1-\nu)/d$ means $k<(d^2+1)/4$.
   605	\end{proof}
