  2010	\item \emph{Post-processing.} $\mathrm T_{\rm post}=M_{\rm rows}^2(\log N)^{O(1)}+B^{1+o(1)}=L^{2a+o(1)}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:lv6}).
  2011	\end{itemize}
  2012	Hence the expected time is at most $L^{o(1)}(L^{a+o(1)}L^{\max(1/\sqrt2,\theta/(2a))}+L^{2a})$. With $a=\theta/\sqrt2$ the first two exponents
  2013	both equal $(1+\theta)/\sqrt2$, and $2a=\sqrt2\theta<(1+\theta)/\sqrt2$ because $\theta<1$.
  2014	\end{proof}
  2015	
  2016	\begin{remark}[the choice of $a$]
  2017	For a general smoothness exponent $a$ (with $S^2=L^{\theta/(2a)}$) the same proof gives $L^{c(a)+o(1)}$ with
  2018	$c(a)=\max(2a,a+1/\sqrt2,a+\theta/(2a))$. For $a>\theta/\sqrt2$ the term $a+1/\sqrt2$ exceeds $(1+\theta)/\sqrt2$. On $(0,\theta/\sqrt2)$ the function
  2019	$a+\theta/(2a)$ is decreasing, because $\theta/\sqrt2<\sqrt{\theta/2}$ for $\theta<1$. So $a=\theta/\sqrt2$ is optimal, and the minimum is $(1+\theta)/\sqrt2$.
  2020	\end{remark}
