    90	\begin{document}
    91	
    92	\begin{abstract}
    93	We describe a Las Vegas algorithm which, given any integer $N\ge2$, outputs the complete prime
    94	factorisation of $N$, and we give a proof that for every $c>1/\sqrt2$ its parameters can be fixed so
    95	that its expected running time is $O(L_N[1/2,c])$, where $L_N[1/2,c]=\exp\bigl(c\sqrt{\log N\log\log
    96	N}\bigr)$. The proof uses no unproved hypothesis. The previous best rigorous bound for factoring every integer is
    97	$L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]$, due to Lenstra and Pomerance (1992).
    98	
    99	For each fixed choice of parameters (an odd degree $d$, a number $k$ of large primes, a base exponent
   100	$\mu$ and a slack $\eta_0$) the expected time is $L_N[1/2,(1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)]$, where $\theta=\theta(d,k,\mu,\eta_0)$
   101	is an explicit exponent. For $d=9$ and $k=13$ this is below $1$ ($c\approx0.9972$). The infimum over
   102	fixed parameters at $d=9$ is $1.3/\sqrt2\approx0.9192$, and over all $d$ it is $1/\sqrt2$. These are
   103	infima: we do not prove that a single algorithm of fixed degree attains $(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2+o(1)$.
   104	
   105	The algorithm combines four ingredients. The first is a ``Dixon descent'': a uniformly random square
   106	$r^2\bmod N$ is written as a ratio $u/v$ of two integers of size $\sqrt N$, and each prime of $uv$ is
   107	descended by a special-$q$ relation in a randomised number field sieve of fixed degree. Because the
   108	root $r$ is never used, every dependency is fruitful with probability $1-2^{1-\omega(N)}$. Second,
   109	the halves $u,v$ are completely factored with the Lenstra--Pomerance algorithm at cost $L_{\sqrt
   110	N}[1/2,1]=L_N[1/2,1/\sqrt2]$, rather than being combined. Third, the supply of relations is reduced
   111	to a single averaged estimate, which we prove by the Paley--Zygmund inequality from a first moment (a
   112	restricted Bombieri--Vinogradov theorem for the moduli $\ell\cdot(\text{smooth})$, in the spirit of Lee--Venkatesan) and a second moment. The
   113	second moment is made tractable by randomising the rational side in two dimensions and by a random
   114	prime multiplier $w$ of the resultant, $F(g_0,g_1)=Nw$, which spreads a forced residue class.
   115	It is then proved with divisor switching and the large sieve. Fourth, the number field sieve
   116	post-processing of Buhler--Lenstra--Pomerance and Lee--Venkatesan is carried out rigorously at fixed
   117	degree, and batch smoothness detection removes any dependence on the hyperelliptic curve method.
   118	
