   121	\item Replacing the Lenstra--Pomerance subroutine by a recursive call of the algorithm itself gives
   122	  $L_N[1/2,\sqrt{(1+\sqrt2)\theta}+o(1)]$. The infimum is $0.851$ at $d=9$, and it tends to $0$ as $d$ grows.
   123	\item A restart schedule over such algorithms yields a single Las Vegas algorithm with expected time $L_N[1/2,o(1)]$; no effective
   124	  rate is supplied. This is not $L_N[\alpha]$ for any $\alpha<1/2$.
   125	\item The same relation supply computes discrete logarithms in $\F_p^*$ in expected time $L_p[1/2,(1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)]$, below
   126	  Pomerance's $L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$.
   127	\end{itemize}
   128	\end{abstract}
