   256	\begin{remark}[what is and is not claimed]\label{rem:claims}
   257	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   258	\item \emph{Infimum, not $o(1)$.} At fixed $d$ the value $(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2$ is an infimum over fixed
   259	  $(k,\eta_0)$. It is not attained, and we do not claim $L_N[1/2,(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2+o(1)]$ for any fixed
   260	  $d$. That would require letting $k=k(N)\to\infty$. Our constants depend on $k$ through factors
   261	  $(\log N)^{O(k)}$, $\binom{2k}{k}$ and thresholds $N_0(k)$, and we have not carried out such a
   262	  diagonalisation. At $d=9$ the first parameter choice below $1$ ($k=13$) has $c\approx0.9972+\eta_0/\sqrt2$; larger fixed $k$ give smaller constants (Table~\ref{tab:constants}), but none attains $0.9192$.
   263	  (Corollary~\ref{cor:P} obtains a genuine $+o(1)$ by scheduling instead.)
   264	\item \emph{Complete factorisation.} The algorithm outputs the full factorisation, with every prime
   265	  certified (AKS \cite{aks2004}). Prime powers, perfect powers and small prime factors are handled before the
   266	  main splitting routine. The splitting routine is applied at most $\log_2N$ times
   267	  (Section~\ref{sec:uniform}).
   268	\item \emph{No hypotheses, no exceptional set.} Nothing is assumed beyond published theorems. In
   269	  particular no Riemann hypothesis, no hyperelliptic curve method (whose published proof trail is
   270	  incomplete), and no exceptional set of $N$. Every input is a published, numbered statement. One of them,
   271	  Drappeau's Lemme~5, has a published proof that points to computations in Harper's unpublished preprint
   272	  (Appendix~\ref{app:85}). The threshold beyond which the time bound holds involves Stark's, Landau's and
   273	  Lagarias--Odlyzko's effective constants; it is enormous, and effective provided the constants of Drappeau's Lemme~5 are
   274	  (which we believe but no published source states). The algorithm itself uses no analytic constant (Remark~\ref{rem:noN0}),
   275	  so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is unaffected either way. The bound holds for every $N$, not on average.
   276	\item \emph{Status.} The proof is new and has not been peer reviewed. It is long and rests on several
   277	  delicate interfaces, so it should be treated with caution until experts have checked it.
   278	\end{enumerate}
   279	\end{remark}
   280	
   281	\subsection{Further consequences: the recursive shape test and discrete logarithms}
   282	The subroutine that costs $L_N[1/2,1/\sqrt2]$ can be replaced by the algorithm itself, called recursively on the two halves. Section~\ref{sec:recursive}
   283	derives the following from the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.\footnote{Theorem~\ref{thm:RA} and Corollary~\ref{cor:P} are conditional on
   284	Theorem~\ref{thm:main}'s proof. They were audited internally by three blind referees (one GPT-6.1-sol, two Claude), all of whom returned
   285	``accept with fixes'' with no fatal or major finding. They are not peer reviewed.}
   286	\begin{itemize}
   287	\item \emph{Theorem~\ref{thm:RA}.} At fixed parameters the expected time is $L_N[1/2,\sqrt{(1+\sqrt2)\theta}+o(1)]$. The infimum over $(k,\eta_0)$ is
   288	  $\sqrt{3(1+\sqrt2)/(d+1)}$, which is $0.851$ at $d=9$ and $0.777$ at $d=11$, and tends to $0$ as $d\to\infty$. These are infima; for example
   289	  $d=9$, $k=13$ gives $0.9952$.
   290	\item \emph{Corollary~\ref{cor:P}.} A restart schedule over such algorithms gives a single algorithm with expected time
   291	  $L_N[1/2,o(1)]$; no effective rate is supplied. The same device turns the infimum $0.9192$ at $d=9$ into a genuine $+o(1)$.
   292	\end{itemize}
   293	Neither gives a rigorous $L_N[\alpha]$ with $\alpha<1/2$ (Section~\ref{sec:limits}). Section~\ref{sec:dlog} transfers the relation supply to
   294	discrete logarithms in $\F_p^*$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:DL}): expected time $L_p[1/2,(1+\theta)/\sqrt2+o(1)]$ whenever $\theta\le1/2$. This beats Pomerance's
   295	rigorous $L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$ \cite{pomerance1987}, which is the best bound we found for all primes.
   296	
