   964	$\cS^2$ proportional to $(u,v)$. Classify $(u,v)$ by the number $i$ of primes that $u$ and $v$ share. Then
   965	$(u,v)=(ju_1,jv_1)$ with $j$ the product of the $i$ shared primes. There are at most $\binom ni\binom n{k-i}^2$ such pairs,
   966	and each has at most $\binom ni$ partners $j'(u_1,v_1)$. Now
   967	\[
   968	\binom ni\binom n{k-i}=\binom ki\binom nk\prod_{t<i}\frac{n-t}{n-k+i-t}\le\binom ki\binom nk\Bigl(1+\frac k{n-k}\Bigr)^k\le\sqrt2\binom ki\binom nk,
   969	\]
   970	since $k^2/(n-k)\le1/3$ for $n\ge4k^2$ and $e^{1/3}<\sqrt2$. So the $i$-th class contributes at most
   971	$2\binom ki^2\binom nk^2\le8k^2\binom ki^2|\cS|^2$. Summing, with $\sum_i\binom ki^2=\binom{2k}k$, gives $\sum n(R)^2\le c_k|\cS|^2$, and
   972	Cauchy--Schwarz finishes the proof as before.
   973	\end{proof}
