   990	\section{Fruitfulness: Dixon descent}\label{sec:DD}
   991	%======================================================================
   992	
   993	\begin{theorem}[Dixon descent; Rabin/Dixon fibre symmetry]\label{thm:DD}
   994	Let $N$ be odd with $\omega(N)\ge2$. Let $r_1,r_2,\dots$ be independent and uniform on $[1,N-1]$, let $R_i:=r_i^2\bmod N$, and let
   995	$\xi$ be further randomness independent of $(r_i)$. Let $\cF:=\sigma((R_i)_i,\xi)$. Let $\cE\in\cF$ be an event on which
   996	an $\cF$-measurable nonempty finite set $\sS$ of indices with every $R_i$ ($i\in\sS$) a unit, and an $\cF$-measurable
   997	$\sY\in(\Z/N)^\times$, are given, with
   998	$\sX^2\equiv\sY^2\pmod N$ where $\sX:=\prod_{i\in\sS}r_i$. Then, conditional on $\cF$, on $\cE$ the ratio $\sX/\sY$ is uniform on $\bmu$, and
   999	\[
  1000	\Prob\bigl[1<\gcd(\sX-\sY,N)<N\,\big|\,\cF\bigr]=1-2^{1-\omega(N)}\ge\tfrac12\qquad\text{on }\cE.
  1001	\]
  1002	\end{theorem}
  1003	
  1004	\begin{proof}
  1005	For every unit square $R$ fix a root $\rho(R)$. Conditional on $R_i$ (a unit), $r_i=\epsilon_i\rho(R_i)$ with $\epsilon_i$ uniform on
  1006	$\bmu$, because each unit square has exactly $|\bmu|$ roots and $r_i$ is uniform. These $\epsilon_i$ are independent of
  1007	each other and of $\xi$. So, conditional on $\cF$, they are i.i.d.\ uniform on $\bmu$. On $\cE$,
  1008	$\sX/\sY=\prod_{i\in\sS}\epsilon_i\cdot\prod_{i\in\sS}\rho(R_i)\sY^{-1}$, where the second factor is $\cF$-measurable and lies in
  1009	$\bmu$ (both $\sX/\sY$ and the first factor do). A product of independent uniform elements of a finite group over a nonempty
  1010	index set is uniform, so $\sX/\sY$ is uniform on $\bmu$. Finally, for $\zeta\in\bmu$ and the
  1011	factorisation $N=\prod p_j^{e_j}$ we have $\zeta\equiv\pm1\pmod{p_j^{e_j}}$ for each $j$, and $\zeta-1$ is a unit modulo
  1012	$p_j^{e_j}$ when $\zeta\equiv-1$ there ($N$ is odd). Hence $\gcd(\sX-\sY,N)=\gcd(\zeta-1,N)=\prod_{\zeta\equiv1\,(p_j^{e_j})}p_j^{e_j}$ with
  1013	$\zeta=\sX/\sY$, which is proper unless $\zeta=\pm1$. These are $2$ of the $2^{\omega(N)}$ elements.
  1014	\end{proof}
  1015	
  1016	\begin{remark}[transcript invariance in Algorithm~\ref{alg:split}]\label{rem:T}
  1017	We check that SPLIT satisfies the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:DD} in each round. Every
  1018	quantity computed before $\sX_\sS$ is a function of the squares $R_i$ and of coins independent of the roots. This covers:
  1019	\begin{itemize}
  1020	\item the field $(g,w,c)$ and its checks;
  1021	\item the Gauss reduction of $\Lambda_{R_i}$ (with a fixed tie rule) and the Lenstra--Pomerance factorisation of $|u_0|,|v_0|$,
  1022	  whose coins are fresh;
  1023	\item the shape decision, and the special-$q$ searches with their cache, which depend on $(A,\ell)$ only;
  1024	\item the gcd checks (the unit status of $r_i$ is that of $R_i$);
  1025	\item all columns, characters, kernel vectors, the Hensel prime, square-root branches, the choice among three dependencies, and
  1026	  $\sY_\sS$ (Section~\ref{sec:lv6}).
  1027	\end{itemize}
  1028	The roots enter only through $\sX_\sS$. After a trivial gcd, all rows are discarded and the next round
  1029	uses fresh roots. For that round we apply Theorem~\ref{thm:DD} with $\xi$ containing everything from earlier rounds, including the
  1030	old roots, which are independent of the new ones. Field retention biases the law of the field and of
  1031	the accepted $R_i$, but not of the fibre signs $\epsilon_i$.
  1032	
  1033	Two cautions. (i) Exactness needs $r$ uniform on all of $[1,N-1]$. Drawing from $[2,N-2]$, for instance, breaks it: at $N=15$,
  1034	$R=1$, the remaining roots $4,11$ both give proper factors. (ii) Accepted roots are \emph{not} uniform on $(\Z/N)^\times$,
  1035	because acceptance filters $R$. What holds, and what is used, is uniformity on the fibre $r_i\bmu$ given $\cF$.
  1036	\end{remark}
