  1595	\subsection{Relation supply}
  1596	
  1597	\begin{theorem}[relation supply]\label{thm:Dk}
  1598	For $N\ge N_0$, $\gamma=\Prob_{A,\ell}[N_\ell(A)\ge1]\ge L^{-o(1)}$. More precisely, with $m:=S^2\rr\rF=L^{o(1)}$ and a fixed polylogarithm $\mathcal P=(\log N)^{C}$,
  1599	$\gamma\ge1/(\mathcal P^3+\mathcal P/m)$.
  1600	\end{theorem}
  1601	
  1602	\begin{proof}
  1603	By Cauchy--Schwarz (Paley--Zygmund) for the nonnegative integer variable $N_\ell(A)$ under the product law of $(\ell,A)$,
  1604	$\gamma\ge(\Ex N)^2/\Ex N^2=(\Ex N)^2/(\Ex N(N-1)+\Ex N)$. The function $z\mapsto z^2/(K+z)$ is increasing on $z>0$. By
  1605	Theorems~\ref{thm:FM} and~\ref{thm:LD}, $\Ex N\ge m/\mathcal P$ and $\Ex N(N-1)\le\mathcal Pm^2$. So
  1606	$\gamma\ge(m/\mathcal P)^2/(\mathcal Pm^2+m/\mathcal P)$. Finally $m=L^{o(1)}$ by \eqref{eq:rhosizes}.
  1607	\end{proof}
  1608	
  1609	\begin{remark}
  1610	$m$ is in general smaller than every negative power of $\log N$: the leading terms of $\log(1/\rr)+\log(1/\rF)$ and $\log S^2$
  1611	cancel, but the second-order terms of the de Bruijn expansion do not. So $\gamma\ge1/(\log N)^{O(1)}$ need not hold for our
  1612	explicit $S$. Only $\gamma\ge L^{-o(1)}$ is used.
  1613	\end{remark}
  1614	
  1615	\subsection{A rational pair correlation (not used in the proof)}
  1616	
  1617	For comparison we record the purely rational version of Step~5, which explains why the rational side is randomised in two
  1618	dimensions. Put $R_1:=\Ex_{\ell,g}\sum_PY_P$ and $R_2:=\Ex_{\ell,g}\sum_{P\ne P'}Y_PY_{P'}$ (sums over $\cP_\ell\cap\Lambda_\ell(g)$).
  1619	
  1620	\begin{proposition}[``Theorem R'']\label{prop:R}
  1621	$R_1\ge S^2\rr/(\log N)^C$ and $R_2\le(\log N)^CS^4\rr^2$.
  1622	\end{proposition}
  1623	
  1624	\begin{proof}
  1625	The lower bound is the rational part of Theorem~\ref{thm:FM}. For the upper bound fix $\ell$, $P$ and $P'$ with $|\Delta|=\ell q$. The map
  1626	$g\mapsto(n,n')=(G_g(P)/\ell,G_g(P')/\ell)$ on $\{g:\ell\mid G_g(P)\}$ is injective, because the matrix $\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}a&-b\\a'&-b'\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$
  1627	has determinant $-\Delta\ne0$. Its image is the lattice $\Gamma_q=\{(n,n'):n'\equiv\lambda n\ (q)\}$, $\lambda\equiv b'b^{-1}$, of index $q$
  1628	($b>2S^2\ge q$ is prime, so $b$ is a unit modulo $q$). Indeed $bG_g(P')-b'G_g(P)=-g_1\Delta$, so the image lies in $\Gamma_q$, and both lattices have index $q$.
  1629	The image of $\cG$ lies in a parallelogram $\Pi$ of area $M^2q/(4\ell)$ inside $[-\Xr,\Xr]^2$, with one side of length $\asymp MV/\ell$ in a direction
  1630	making an angle in $[\arctan\frac12,\arctan2]$ with the axes. Cover $\Pi$ by $O(qS^2)$ squares of side $w_0=M/V$. In a square $I_1\times I_2$
  1631	away from the axes, Lemma~\ref{lem:DS} in each coordinate (modulus $q$, length $w_0\ge\Xr/(4S^2)$, since $V\VM\le2YS^2$) gives at most
  1632	$\sum_{r\bmod q}\bigl((w_0/q)\rr(\log N)^C\bigr)^2=(w_0^2/q)\rr^2(\log N)^{2C}$ smooth points of $\Gamma_q$. Near the axes the filter
  1633	$|n|,|n'|\ge\Xr/256$ removes everything. So each pair has at most $(M^2/\ell)\rr^2(\log N)^C$ admissible $g$. There are at most $200S^2$
  1634	partners $P'$ per $P$: they lie in the lattice $\{P':\ell\mid\det(P,P')\}$, which is $\Lambda_\ell(g)$ for $g=(a,b)$ and contains $P\in\cP_\ell$, so it is regular and Lemma~\ref{lem:reg} applies, and $\#\cP_\ell\le CV^2$. Dividing by $|\cG|\asymp M^2$ gives
  1635	$R_2\le CV^2S^2\ell^{-1}\rr^2(\log N)^C\le(\log N)^CS^4\rr^2$.
  1636	\end{proof}
  1637	
  1638	\noindent In the one-dimensional design $G=x-my$ the rational values $(s,s')$ of a pair move on a \emph{line}
  1639	$b's-bs'=\Delta/\ell$ as $m$ varies. Then divisor switching reduces to counting solutions of one linear equation in a box,
  1640	which has no area main term, and we know no bound of the needed strength. In two dimensions the pair fills a lattice region of
  1641	area much larger than its perimeter times the lattice scale, and that is all the argument needs.
