  1134	\begin{lemma}[one-point smoothing]\label{lem:smooth1}
  1135	Let $P=(a,b)$ with $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $ab\ne0$, put $V'=\max(|a|,|b|)$ and $l_i=|a|^{d-1-i}|b|^i$, and let $H\ge3dV'$. For $c$ uniform on
  1136	$C_H$ there is a probability measure $\vartheta$ on $\Z$ such that:
  1137	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  1138	\item $\|\mathrm{Law}(\langle c,v(P)\rangle)-\vartheta\|_{\rm TV}\le3d^2V'/(2H+1)$;
  1139	\item $\vartheta$ is the law of $\sum_iY_i$ with $Y_i$ independent and uniform on integer intervals of lengths $(2H+1)l_i$;
  1140	\item $\vartheta(z)$ is a non-increasing function of $|z-M_0|$ for some $M_0\in\frac12\Z$ with $|M_0|\le dV'^{d-1}$;
  1141	\item $\max_z\vartheta(z)\le1/((2H+1)V'^{d-1})$.
  1142	\end{enumerate}
  1143	\end{lemma}
  1144	
  1145	\begin{proof}
  1146	Write $v=v(P)$. Its entries have $\gcd(a^{d-1},b^{d-1})=1$. The vectors $u_j:=b\,e_j-a\,e_{j+1}$ ($0\le j\le d-2$)
  1147	lie in $\ker\langle\cdot,v\rangle$. The maximal minor of the matrix with rows $u_j$ that omits column $i$ equals
  1148	$\pm a^{d-1-i}b^i$, so the gcd of the maximal minors is $1$, and the $u_j$ are a basis of the lattice
  1149	$K:=\{x\in\Z^d:\langle x,v\rangle=0\}$. Each coordinate of a point $\sum s_ju_j$ with $s\in[0,1)^{d-1}$ has absolute value at most
  1150	$|a|+|b|\le2V'$.
  1151	
  1152	\emph{Representations.} Let $m\in\Z$ with $|m|\le\|v\|_2$. Since $\gcd$ of the entries is $1$, the integer solutions of
  1153	$\langle x,v\rangle=m$ form a coset $x_0+K$. The real point $p_m:=mv/\|v\|_2^2$ lies on the same affine hyperplane and
  1154	has $\|p_m\|_\infty\le|m|/\|v\|_2\le1$. Writing $p_m-x_0=\sum s_ju_j$ and $x:=x_0+\sum\lfloor s_j\rfloor u_j$, we get
  1155	$\langle x,v\rangle=m$ and $\|x\|_\infty\le1+2V'$.
  1156	
  1157	\emph{Smoothing.} Since $l_i\le V'^{d-1}\le\|v\|_2$, for each $i$ we may fix a set $S_i$ of $l_i$ vectors with
  1158	$\|x\|_\infty\le3V'$ on which $\langle\cdot,v\rangle$ is a bijection onto $\{0,\dots,l_i-1\}$. Let $x^{(i)}$ be uniform on $S_i$,
  1159	independent of each other and of $c$, and put $c':=c+\sum_ix^{(i)}$. Then
  1160	$\langle c',v\rangle=\sum_iY_i$ with $Y_i:=c_iv_i+\langle x^{(i)},v\rangle$. Here $c_iv_i=\pm c_il_i$ with $\pm c_i$ uniform on
  1161	$[-H,H]$, so $Y_i$ is uniform on an integer interval of length $(2H+1)l_i$. The $Y_i$ are independent. Let $\vartheta$ be the
  1162	law of $\sum Y_i$. For a fixed shift $y$, the uniform measures on $C_H$ and $C_H+y$ differ in total variation by
  1163	at most $\|y\|_1/(2H+1)$. Hence $\|\mathrm{Law}(c)-\mathrm{Law}(c')\|_{\rm TV}\le d\cdot3dV'/(2H+1)$, and (a) follows by
  1164	applying the map $\langle\cdot,v\rangle$.
  1165	
  1166	(c) A uniform distribution on an integer interval is a log-concave sequence, symmetric about the midpoint of the
  1167	interval. Convolutions of log-concave sequences without internal zeros are log-concave (see e.g.\
  1168	\cite{stanley1989}), and a log-concave sequence is unimodal. A symmetric unimodal sequence is
  1169	non-increasing in the distance to its centre. The centre of $Y_i$ is $(l_i-1)/2$ for either sign of $v_i$, so $|M_0|\le\sum l_i/2\le dV'^{d-1}/2$.
